:"2026 so far: 12 daily record highs, no daily record lows","records":"Grand Island has nearly stopped setting record lows","change":"Grand Island's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days show no clear change.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 47\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13: a typical high, but a cold night","p_yest":"The high of <strong>47\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 52\u00b0F, and the record is 82\u00b0F (1999). The low of <strong>20\u00b0F</strong> was the 21st coldest November 13 low (tied) of 132 since 1895.","t_rec":"Grand Island has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Grand Island's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Grand Island has set <strong class=\"sig\">36 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 75 that chance alone would give, and <strong>86 record highs</strong>, close to the 76 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (90). Grand Island's all-time high is 117\u00b0F, on July 24, 1936.","t_chg":"Grand Island's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days show no clear change.","p_chg":"Across 125 complete years: Grand Island's average low shows no clear change: +0.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.15) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Grand Island's average high shows no clear change: +0.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.08 to +0.13) that includes zero. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.48\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.13 to +0.83) and highs show no clear change. The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 2 of 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months; July and August fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Grand Island's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.10 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 26.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 1905 (45.50 in); the driest, 2012 (11.55 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 12 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}